body.query:
query BlockingLinks($owner: String!, $name: String!, $cursor: String) {
  repository(owner: $owner, name: $name) {
    issues(states: OPEN, first: 50, after: $cursor) {
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
      nodes {
        number
        blockedBy(first: 50) {
          nodes { number state closedAt repository { nameWithOwner } }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

query ClosingRefs($owner: String!, $name: String!, $cursor: String) {
  repository(owner: $owner, name: $name) {
    issues(states: OPEN, first: 50, after: $cursor) {
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
      nodes {
        number
        closedByPullRequestsReferences(first: 25, includeClosedPrs: true) {
          totalCount
          nodes {
            number title state mergedAt headRefName baseRefName
            mergeCommit { oid }
            repository { nameWithOwner }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

query MergedChanges($owner: String!, $name: String!, $cursor: String) {
  repository(owner: $owner, name: $name) {
    pullRequests(states: MERGED, first: 50, after: $cursor,
                 orderBy: {field: UPDATED_AT, direction: DESC}) {
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
      nodes {
        number title url headRefName isDraft createdAt updatedAt mergedAt
        author { __typename login }
        mergeCommit { oid }
        closingIssuesReferences(first: 25) { nodes { number repository { nameWithOwner } } }
      }
    }
  }
}

query OpenChanges($owner: String!, $name: String!, $cursor: String) {
  repository(owner: $owner, name: $name) {
    pullRequests(states: OPEN, first: 50, after: $cursor,
                 orderBy: {field: UPDATED_AT, direction: DESC}) {
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
      nodes {
  number title url headRefName isDraft mergeable reviewDecision createdAt updatedAt mergedAt
  author { __typename login }
  mergeCommit { oid }
  assignees(first: 50) { nodes { login } }
  reviewRequests(first: 50) {
    nodes { requestedReviewer { __typename ... on User { login } ... on Bot { login }
                                ... on Team { slug } } }
  }
  commits(last: 1) { nodes { commit { oid statusCheckRollup { state } } } }
}
    }
  }
}

response.body:
On lokkju/infra (forge-tracker 2026.10.15.3, `/next`, 2026-10-16), all 40 open issues came out `agent-ready` and "Your queue" was empty. The repo has no `labels.needs_human` or `labels.decision` configured and no issue carries a blocking link, so nothing could mark an item as anything but agent-ready. Many of them can't be done by an agent:

- **physical work:** lokkju/infra#136 (repaste a GPU), lokkju/infra#248 (pull a cable);
- **needs a device or a test window:** lokkju/infra#231, lokkju/infra#9, lokkju/infra#188;
- **research done, waiting on the owner's decision:** lokkju/infra#214, lokkju/infra#204, lokkju/infra#201, lokkju/infra#203, lokkju/infra#211, lokkju/infra#148;
- **waiting on an external event or another repo:** lokkju/infra#267, lokkju/infra#269, lokkju/infra#275, lokkju/infra#197, lokkju/infra#35, lokkju/infra#138;
- **explicitly parked:** lokkju/infra#305, whose title ends "(parked)".

So the classifier's default treats a missing signal as "an agent can do this". With no labels configured, that default is wrong for most real backlogs, and the report reads as a confident queue rather than an unclassified one. The ranking step then has to quietly leave such items out of lanes, which only the slate note records.

Expected (any of these would fix it):
1. **With no needs-human or decision label configured, classify `unknown` instead of `agent-ready`,** and say so in the report header. The facts schema already has `unknown`.
2. **Read cheap text signals** in the title and body: "(parked)", "blocked on", "waits on", "physically", "decision", "the operator decides", "after <issue>" with a link. Mark matches `unknown` or `person-only`, with the matched phrase as the marker.
3. **Let the slate move items into the queue.** `save-slate` accepts `overrules` only to move person-only items into lanes; it can't move an agent-ready item into "Your queue" with a reason. Allow the reverse overrule (item to queue, with a reason).

Also seen: the "Blocked" section said "nothing blocked" although several items name what they wait on in their body. With no blocking links that's technically true, but it should say "no blocking links recorded" rather than imply nothing is blocked.

The TaskUpdate hook passed a new subject straight through, so a revised item written as a draft (`DECIDE: ... (recommend X) [link]`) was stored without its `D<n>`/`A<n>` and came back from the task files as `D?`. A label typed back in by hand wasn't checked either.

The hook now settles the subject before the tool runs, through a pure `retitle()` in `hooks/queue.ts`:

- a draft subject on an existing item is rewritten to `formatSubject(<its label>, draft)`;
- a subject that carries a label must carry the item's own label, or the update is refused with `session-decisions: task <id> is <label>; keep its label or drop it and the hook adds it`;
- `problems()` runs on every decision subject, so a revised DECIDE still needs its recommendation and link;
- a task that wasn't an item, an item the hook never labelled (`D?`), or an item whose kind changes gets a fresh label, the way TaskCreate does it;
- a typed label on a task that wasn't an item is accepted only if no other item holds it and its letter fits the kind.

Tests: `retitle()` cases in `tests/queue.test.ts`; in `tests/register.test.tsx`, a retitle without a label keeps it, a matching label goes through, a mismatched label and a missing recommendation are refused and leave the task alone, and a plain task retitled into a draft becomes `A2` with numbering continuing after it. 25 pass; `tsc` and `claude plugin validate --strict` are clean.

SKILL.md is unchanged: it never told the assistant to retype labels on a revision.

Fixes #7

